According to a new report from IntelMarketResearch, the global electric vehicle market was valued at USD 620 billion in 2025 and is projected to grow from USD 660 billion in 2026 to USD 1.48 trillion by 2034, registering a CAGR of 10.6% during 2026–2034.

The market is experiencing significant expansion as automakers broaden electric vehicle portfolios, battery costs evolve, and governments and commercial fleet operators pursue lower-emission transportation. Increasing availability of battery electric and plug-in hybrid models, together with improvements in charging infrastructure, is supporting adoption across passenger and commercial vehicle categories.

What Is the Electric Vehicle Market?
The electric vehicle market includes newly sold battery electric vehicles (BEVs) and plug-in hybrid electric vehicles (PHEVs) across passenger and commercial road vehicle categories. BEVs operate using rechargeable battery packs, while PHEVs combine an externally chargeable battery system with an internal combustion engine.

The market excludes conventional non-plug-in hybrids, fuel-cell-only vehicles, used-vehicle resales, standalone battery components, and charging-service revenue. Competition is shaped by vehicle performance, battery economics, charging access, model availability, manufacturing scale, and after-sales support.

Key Market Drivers
Lower Battery Costs and Wider Vehicle Availability
Improvements in battery technology and manufacturing scale are supporting the expansion of electric vehicle offerings. As manufacturers introduce models across more price points and vehicle categories, consumers and fleet operators have greater choice when evaluating electric alternatives.

Fleet Electrification and Emissions Requirements
Commercial fleet operators, municipal transport providers, and delivery companies are increasingly evaluating electric vehicles to manage operating costs and emissions. Fleet procurement can support demand for passenger and commercial platforms, particularly where charging and maintenance infrastructure are available.

Expansion of Charging Infrastructure
The development of public, workplace, and residential charging networks is improving vehicle usability. Greater charging access can reduce range-related concerns and support wider adoption among private buyers and fleet operators.

